TY  - JOUR
TI  - Architectural identity in smart city development: a multi-criteria study on regional culture in northeastern China
AU  - Yaqi Zhang
AU  - Chunyu Ji
AU  - Jing Ye
AU  - Heng Zou
AU  - Lanjia Peng
PY  - 2025
JO  - Journal of Asian Architecture and Building Engineering
SP  - 1
EP  - 12
DO  - 10.1080/13467581.2025.2560455
AB  - In northern China, smart city infrastructure development is directly affected by the cultural aspects of the region, and there is a need for a futuristic strategy to make decisions that complement tradition with technology. The current study attempts to show the way how MCDM frameworks and digital twin technology are combined to influence urban planning and infrastructure optimization concerning cultural issues. The current study is about how regional culture influences smart city infrastructure development in northeastern China through a survey-based approach. This research study investigates how cultural values, governance models, and socio-economic conditions influence smart city development by collecting information from urban planners, policymakers, architects, and residents of key cities. The questionnaire aimed to identify stakeholder perceptions regarding integrating traditional architectural elements, local institutional frameworks, and new technologies, such as digital twins and artificial intelligence, into urban planning. The study employs multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) methods to examine the importance of relevance and cultural weightage in the selection of a decision-making framework for development that is aimed at optimizing infrastructure, sustainability, and efficiency. Surveys confirm technology acceptance, but preference toward regional identity through adaptive strategies takes precedence in urban planning. This research shows how computational intelligence aids the differential urban transformation under uncertainty pertaining to decision-making while keeping an eye on culture. Challenges confronting the smart city development processes in the region are those such as change management, gaps in policy, and technological accessibility. The findings suggest that the MCDM’s hybrid decision support systems with stakeholder consultations could enhance smart city project formulation without compromising heritage.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1080/13467581.2025.2560455
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Automated dimension estimation of bridge components using semantic segmentation and geometric fitting of point cloud data
AU  - Yu Chen
AU  - Chao Lin
AU  - Xianfeng Li
AU  - Shiori Kubo
AU  - Tatsuro Yamane
AU  - Pang-jo Chun
PY  - 2025
JO  - Engineering Structures
VL  - 342
SP  - 120837
EP  - 120837
DO  - 10.1016/j.engstruct.2025.120837
AB  - Accurate estimation of dimensions for bridge components is essential for maintaining infrastructure and ensuring safety. This study proposes an automated framework that integrates deep learning-based semantic segmentation with geometric analysis for bridge component dimension estimation from point cloud data. The framework comprises two main stages: (1) a semantic segmentation module based on the PointNet++ architecture with MSG, enhanced with the improved structure-oriented concept (SOC) for improved component recognition, and (2) a geometric processing pipeline that incorporates voxel-based downsampling, RANSAC plane fitting, and multi-stage filtering for robust dimension extraction. The improved SOC mechanism effectively utilizes the inherent spatial relationships of bridge components, significantly enhancing segmentation accuracy in complex structural contexts. The geometric processing pipeline employs an adaptive region-growing algorithm for component isolation, followed by principal component analysis for orientation alignment and dimension calculation. Validation experiments conducted on two bridge datasets of varying scales demonstrated the effectiveness of the framework, achieving an overall relative percentage error (RPE) of 5.05% and a strong correlation (Pearson r = 0.904) between predicted and actual measurements. The method consistently performed well across various components, across two datasets the framework achieved mean relative errors of 2.6 % (length) and 5.2 % (width) for smaller bridges and 6.9 % (length) and 5.5 % (width) for larger bridges, confirming both accuracy and scalability. Statistical Bland–Altman analysis showed minimal systematic bias, underscoring the method’s practical value for automated bridge inspection and digital-twin applications. • Proposed a robust framework for automated bridge component dimension estimation. • Achieved 5.05% RPE with strong scalability across small and large bridge structures. • Integrated multi-stage geometric processing pipeline for noise reduction and accuracy. • Validated framework using two diverse datasets with annotated bridge point clouds. • Adaptive parameter selection ensures consistency across varying structural scales.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engstruct.2025.120837
ER  - 
